# BillDog

- Read the live BillDog project over the `billdog` MCP server before you write a
  product id, entitlement key, offering id, placement identifier or feature-flag
  key into code. Use the identifier the project actually has; never guess one.
- New BillDog items start as drafts or inactive. Publishing a paywall, starting
  or stopping an experiment, turning a feature flag on or changing its rollout,
  and changing a live offering all change what real app users see. Ask the user
  before each of these.
- Queries read production data unless you pass `sandbox: true`. If a production
  read comes back empty, the project may only have sandbox data so far: retry
  with `sandbox: true` and tell the user which environment the numbers came from.
- Put only a public SDK key in client code, never a secret key, and never commit
  a key.
